{
  "summary": "Both previous contributions were partially right but missed the actual root cause. I verified experimentally that Go's html/template engine applies context-aware auto-escaping to attribute values EVEN when the value has type template.HTMLAttr. The escapeAttr function manually encodes (e.g., < to &lt;), then Go's template engine re-encodes (e.g., &lt; to &amp;lt;), producing double-escaped output. For input 'if x < y && z > 0', the rendered attribute becomes 'if x &amp;lt; y &amp;amp;&amp;amp; z &amp;gt; 0'. The browser decodes this once to '&lt;' etc., which is what the user sees as visible entity codes. The fix is to remove the escapeAttr function entirely and use plain Go template interpolation ({{.Plan}} instead of {{escapeAttr .Plan}}), which produces correct single-level escaping. Go auto-escapes &, <, >, and quotes in attribute values. Newlines are valid in quoted HTML attributes and work fine. The JavaScript escapeHtml() must be KEPT — it is the correct and necessary XSS protection for innerHTML rendering after getAttribute() returns the decoded text."
}
